---
// Install, as a doc: the ISO first, because a fresh install is the common
// case; the key enrollment the NVIDIA image needs (nouveau is blacklisted in
// system_files.nvidia, so an unenrolled NVIDIA-only machine has no GPU
// driver); the switch for a machine already on an atomic Fedora; and what the
// first login and updates are like.
// The home page's #install is the short version and links here for the rest.
import Iso from '../../components/Iso.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, IMAGES, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Install Pulsar from the weekly ISO, or switch to it from Fedora Silverblue or Kinoite with bootc switch. How to enroll the NVIDIA key, and how updates work.';

const ENROL = 'sudo mokutil --import /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der\nsudo systemctl reboot';
const TAKE = `mokutil --test-key /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der   # ...is already enrolled\nsudo bootc switch ${IMAGES.nvidia}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const CHECK = 'modinfo -F signer nvidia\nnvidia-smi';
const switchTo = (image: string) => `sudo bootc switch \\\n  ${image}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Install Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/install', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const MOK = ['Enroll MOK', 'View key 0', 'Continue', 'Yes', 'password', 'reboot'];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'iso', label: 'A fresh install', level: 2 },
  { id: 'secure-boot', label: 'NVIDIA: enroll the key, once', level: 2 },
  { id: 'switch', label: 'Switch from an atomic Fedora', level: 2 },
  { id: 'first-login', label: 'When you log in', level: 2 },
  { id: 'updates', label: 'Updates', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Install"
  fullTitle="Install Pulsar"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/install"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    On a new machine you need the ISO and a USB stick. If you’re already on Silverblue, Kinoite or
    another atomic Fedora, you can switch in place with <code>bootc switch</code> and a restart.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iso">A fresh install</h2>
  <Iso />
  <p class="hint">
    If you’re keeping Windows alongside or coming from a Mac, read <a href="/docs/coming-from">From Windows or macOS</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Boot from the stick and start the installer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pick a disk. If it already starts Windows or Linux, you can install alongside it in 36&nbsp;GB of
      free space (the installer counts that as 39&nbsp;GB), or erase it. Nothing is written until the
      button on the last page, but back up first.
    </li>
    <li>Disk encryption is on unless you turn it off. The passphrase unlocks the disk at every start.</li>
    <li>
      When it’s done, press <strong>Restart Now</strong> and pull the stick when the screen goes dark.
      You create your account the first time Pulsar starts.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="ISOs from before August 17 erase disks without asking">
    ISOs published before August 17, 2026 erased every disk they could see (under Ventoy, the stick too)
    without asking. The <code>-latest</code> links above always point at the current build, which asks.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="secure-boot">NVIDIA: enroll the key, once</h2>
  <p>
    The NVIDIA image ships only NVIDIA’s open kernel driver, which needs a GeForce GTX 16 or RTX 20
    series card or newer. The driver is signed with Pulsar’s key, so you can leave Secure&nbsp;Boot on
    after you enroll that key once, through MokManager. The standard image doesn’t need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Until the key is enrolled, the NVIDIA driver doesn’t load, and the image blocks nouveau, the
    community open-source driver. On a machine with integrated graphics the desktop runs on those, and
    shortly after login GNOME Software offers to enroll the key. Follow its prompts, restart, and
    answer MokManager as in step 2. Or enroll it with the steps below. TODO(copy): what does a
    machine whose only GPU is NVIDIA show before the key is enrolled? The OS repo’s
    docs/SIGNING.md says a black screen.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Import the certificate</h3>
      <p><code>mokutil</code> asks for a one-time password you retype at the next boot.</p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="enroll" code={ENROL}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Answer MokManager</h3>
      <p>The next boot stops in MokManager, a blue firmware screen. Go through it in this order, and type the password from step 1 when it asks.</p>
      <p class="mok">
        {MOK.map((step, index) => (
          <>
            <arc-kbd>{step}</arc-kbd>
            {index < MOK.length - 1 && <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
      <p class="hint">If you miss the screen, run the import again.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>If you switched, move to the NVIDIA image</h3>
      <p>Skip this if you installed from the NVIDIA ISO. Otherwise, once the key is in:</p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="switch" code={TAKE}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Check the driver loaded</h3>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="check" code={CHECK}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="If the key gets cleared">
    A BIOS update can clear the MOK list. Accept GNOME Software’s Secure Boot prompt: it enrolls{' '}
    <code>/etc/pki/akmods/certs/public_key.der</code>, which Pulsar keeps identical to{' '}
    <code>MOK.der</code>. Or run the import above again.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="switch">Switch from an atomic Fedora</h2>
  <p>
    From Silverblue, Kinoite or another atomic Fedora, switch in place. Your files and accounts are
    kept, and the old image stays in the boot menu until the next update.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="standard" code={switchTo(IMAGES.vanilla)}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="nvidia" code={switchTo(IMAGES.nvidia)}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    NVIDIA with Secure Boot on: switch to the standard image first,{' '}
    <a href="#secure-boot">enroll the key</a> it ships, then switch to NVIDIA.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-login">When you log in</h2>
  <p>
    New accounts start in Pulsar’s theme, and a welcome window opens where you can pick a look or
    add a coding agent. To reopen it, find <strong>Welcome to Pulsar</strong>
    in the app grid. A customized account you switched keeps its look.
  </p>
  <p>
    Default apps (Steam, Heroic, video and music players, the GNOME set and more) install on the
    first boot with a network. <code>sudo pulsar setup apps</code> reinstalls any that are missing.
  </p>
  <p>If something looks wrong, run a health check.</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="health" code="pulsar doctor"></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/docs/where-things-go">Where things go</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
  <p>
    New builds download in the background and apply at your next restart. <code>sudo pulsar
    update</code> downloads one now, and <code>sudo pulsar rollback</code> returns to the previous
    build. <a href="/docs/updates">Updates and rollback</a> has the details.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-xl);
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .steps > li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 3rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .steps > li::before {
    counter-increment: step;
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    text-shadow: var(--glow-xs);
    line-height: var(--heading-lh);
  }
  .steps h3 {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
  }
  .mok {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--text-ghost);
  }
</style>
